<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qin Shi Huang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">259–<a href="/years/210-bce">210 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Ying Zheng, King Zheng of Qin, First Emperor of Qin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> Shi Huang, born Ying Zheng, became king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> in 246 BCE and between 230 and 221 BCE conquered the six rival Warring States, unifying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He took the new title Huangdi, emperor, and is known as the First Emperor. Advised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a>, he standardised script, coinage, weights and laws and built roads and walls. His tomb was guarded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang.jpg" alt="Qin Shi Huang" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (18??–18??) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:QinShiHuang19century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Qin Shi Huang was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a>, the capital of Zhao, in 259 BCE as Ying Zheng or Zhao Zheng. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, he was the eldest son of the Qin prince Yiren, a hostage at the court of Zhao who later became King Zhuangxiang of Qin. The Shiji also claims that he was the actual son of the merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-buwei">Lü Buwei</a>. Modern scholars, among them Derk Bodde, doubt this and think the story was added to slander him.</p><p>King Zhuangxiang died in 246 BCE and was succeeded by his 13-year-old son. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-buwei">Lü Buwei</a> acted as regent until 235 BCE, when Zhao Zheng assumed full power after Lü was banished for his involvement in a scandal with Queen Dowager Zhao. Lao Ai's attempted coup of 238 BCE failed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a> became the new chancellor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>By 221 BCE he had conquered all the other warring states and unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and he took the new title huángdì rather than reuse the existing title for king. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a> he organised the empire into 36 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a>, later 40, then counties, townships and hundred-family units, with appointments based on merit instead of hereditary right. They standardised weights and measures, the length of wagon axles and the currency, which became the Ban Liang coin, and the seal script of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> became the standard for a universal written language. His public works included a national road system and the incorporation of the walls of several states into a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>.</p><p>He eliminated the Hundred Schools of Thought, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legalism">Legalism</a> became the mandatory ideology. He is traditionally said to have banned and burned many books and executed scholars, and he fell ill and died during his fifth tour of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The emperor died in July or August of 210 BCE at Shaqiu, aged 49. He had never written a will. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a> and the eunuch Zhao Gao concealed his death, then forged a letter commanding his eldest son Fusu to commit suicide, and his younger son Hu Hai became the Second Emperor. His mausoleum, begun in 215 BCE, is guarded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army</a>.</p><p>Traditional Chinese historiography portrayed him as a brutal tyrant. Since the mid-20th century scholars have questioned this view, which stems partly from the harsh assessments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qin Shi Huang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 214 BCE <b>Qin Shi Huang</b> kept roughly 100,000 men on the northern border and sent some 500,000 south against the Baiyue peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Qi traditions of sacrifice at Mount Tai and of the quest for immortality later drew the <b>First Emperor of Qin</b> and Emperor Wu of Han to Shandong.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He then adopted the new title of First Emperor (<b>Shi Huangdi</b>), and the kingdom became the Qin dynasty, ruling all of China from Xianyang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>First Emperor of Qin</b> unified China in 221 BCE, his minister Li Si standardised the script, which had developed regional variants, and the Han dynasty that followed made the classics the foundation of education.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…(twelve chapters) resemble the court chronicles of earlier tradition: the first five cover periods such as the Five Emperors or dynasties such as the Xia, Shang and Zhou, and the last seven cover individual rulers from the <b>First Emperor of Qin</b> to the first emperors of the Han.</blockquote><a href="/work/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The Terracotta Army belongs to the mausoleum of <b>Qin Shi Huang</b>, the first emperor of China.</blockquote><a href="/work/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Guangzong County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.61,114.49&amp;year=-228" aria-label="Handan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Qin Shi Huang"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4622 4427h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4622 4427h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.61,114.49&amp;year=-228">What was here in 228 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
